Assistant: The error occurs because we're trying to make actual HTTP requests with the TRPC client. Instead, we should create a completely mocked version that doesn't try to make real network requests. Let me fix this by creating a proper mock setup:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/AdminOrgTable-bd0a7fbc.tsx">
  <change>
    <description>Replace TRPC setup with a fully mocked version that doesn't make network requests</description>
    <content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import ImportedComponent from '../../../../packages/features/ee/organizations/pages/settings/admin/AdminOrgPage';
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';

// Create a mock client
const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      refetchOnWindowFocus: false,
      retry: false,
    },
  },
});

// Create a mock TRPC context
const createMockTrpc = (mockData) => ({
  useContext: () => ({
    viewer: {
      organizations: {
        adminGetAll: {
          invalidate: async () => {},
        },
        adminGet: {
          invalidate: async () => {},
          refetch: async () => {},
        },
      },
    },
  }),
  viewer: {
    organizations: {
      adminGetAll: {
        useSuspenseQuery: () => [mockData],
        useQuery: () => ({ data: mockData, isLoading: false, error: null }),
      },
      adminUpdate: {
        useMutation: () => ({
          mutate: async () => {},
          isLoading: false,
        }),
      },
      adminDelete: {
        useMutation: () => ({
          mutate: async () => {},
          isLoading: false,
        }),
      },
    },
  },
});

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    mockData: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify([
        {
          id: 1,
          name: "Sample Organization",
          slug: "sample-org",
          members: [{ user: { email: "user@example.com" } }],
          organizationSettings: {
            isAdminReviewed: false,
            isOrganizationConfigured: false,
            isAdminAPIEnabled: false,
          },
          metadata: { requestedSlug: "sample-org" },
        },
      ]),
      label: "Mock Data",
    },
  });

  // Parse the mock data
  const mockData = JSON.parse(state.mockData.value);

  // Create mock TRPC instance
  const mockTrpc = createMockTrpc(mockData);

  // Create a mock Provider component
  const MockTRPCProvider = ({ children }) => {
    return children;
  };

  // Override the global trpc object
  const trpcContext = {
    ...mockTrpc,
    Provider: MockTRPCProvider,
  };

  return (
    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
      {/* @ts-ignore */}
      <trpcContext.Provider>
        <ImportedComponent />
      </trpcContext.Provider>
    </QueryClientProvider>
  );
}
</content>
  </change>
</file>

The changes I made include:

1. Removed the actual TRPC client setup that was trying to make network requests
2. Created a fully mocked TRPC context that returns static data
3. Created a simple pass-through Provider component instead of using the real TRPC Provider
4. Added more complete mock implementations including useQuery variants
5. Simplified the overall structure to avoid any actual network requests
6. Made sure all necessary TRPC methods are mocked with proper return types

This should resolve the "Failed to fetch" error because we're no longer attempting to make any real network requests. Instead, we're providing all the necessary data and methods through our mock implementation.

The mock now provides:
- All required TRPC methods
- Proper data structure matching what the component expects
- No actual network requests
- Proper query client context for React Query

This should allow the component to render properly without any network-related errors.

